 "final": {
  "reply_raw": "{\"mode\": \"precedent\", \"rule\": {\"action\": \"read_restricted_file\", \"resource\": \"file-hr-comp\", \"conditions\": {\"purpose\": [\"eq\", \"incident\"], \"ticket_open\": [\"eq\", \"1\"], \"classification\": [\"le\", \"1\"], \"ttl_min\": [\"le\", \"60\"]}}}\n\nI'm proposing a standing rule here. Rationale: the same shape has recurred 10 times — a security-incident read of `file-hr-comp`, always with an open ticket and an incident purpose — so a case per read is unnecessary friction.\n\nI deliberately scoped it narrowly rather than using an open resource or `any` conditions:\n\n- **resource fixed** to `file-hr-comp`, not `*`, so no other restricted file is covered.\n- **purpose = incident** and **ticket_open = 1** — the two conditions that make the access accountable; a read without an open incident ticket still falls back to a case.\n- **classification ≤ 1** and **ttl_min ≤ 60** bound the request to the highest classification and longest access window actually observed, so escalation beyond that needs separate authorization.\n\nIf you'd rather keep per-case adjudication, say so and I'll continue in `case` mode.",
